                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Adds an axis of length 1 to a tensor's shape, at the position you choose, leaving the numbers alone.\n\nTwelve values as [12] are a list. As [1, 12] they are a table of one row; as [12, 1], a table of one column. Same numbers, but a node that wants a 2-D input, or a batch of inputs, needs one of the latter. t.unsqueeze makes it.\n\nWHERE THE NEW AXIS GOES:\ndim 0 puts the 1 in front: [12] becomes [1, 12], a single row - the usual way to make a \"batch of one\". dim 1 (for a 1-D tensor, the end) puts it behind: [12, 1], a column. A negative dim counts from the end: -1 always puts the new axis last, and the most negative one allowed puts it first. For a [7, 6] tensor the choices are 0, 1 and 2 (or -3, -2 and -1), giving [1, 7, 6], [7, 1, 6] and [7, 6, 1]. For a [12] row they are 0 and 1, or -2 and -1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.unsqueeze <dim: int>\nA bare t.unsqueeze always uses dim 0 and has no dim box; give an\nargument to get the box.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.unsqueeze 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet - only when an argument was given):\nWhere to insert the axis: from minus (the number of axes + 1) up to the number of axes. A dim outside that range sends nothing. Changing it does NOT send by itself; in the demo the metro re-sends the ramp, so the picture follows.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe same numbers with one more axis, of length 1.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe ramp arrives as [12], a row. t.unsqueeze 1 makes it [12, 1]: twelve rows of one value, and the heat_map draws a column. Type 0 into dim and it is [1, 12] - one row of twelve, which looks like the input again but is now 2-D (t.info shows the extra 1). -1 is the same as 1 here, and -2 the same as 0.\n\nRELATED:\nt.squeeze removes length-1 axes - the reverse. t.reshape can set any shape, including these. t.stack adds a new axis while joining several tensors. np.expand_dims (np.unsqueeze) is the numpy version."
        },
